<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Act of Union 1840</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as British North America Act 1840</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Act of Union 1840 was a British statute that merged Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and Lower Canada into the single Province of Canada, proclaimed in February 1841. It followed the rebellions of 1837 and 1838 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lambton-1st-earl-of-durham">Lord Durham</a>'s report, which aimed to assimilate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canadians</a>. Both halves received equal seats in one legislature, but political deadlock between them led to Confederation in 1867.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/act-of-union-1840.jpg" alt="Act of Union 1840" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Coat_of_arms_of_Canada.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
